+### The landing backdrop replaced, 29 Oct 2026 (branch `feat/demo-polish`)
+
+`hero-backdrop.jpeg` (a photo collage added in c92f907 on 9 Sept, kept in `public/` and `src/assets/`) was replaced by `frontend/public/hero-backdrop.svg`. It was row 1 of the owner's feature table.
+
+- **Why.** No source was ever recorded for the photo, and it showed US dollar notes in a rupee product. The repo went public under MIT on 28 to 29 Oct, and the MIT licence cannot cover a picture nobody can attribute. The replacement is drawn in code, so it falls under MIT and shows the brand's own lotus and rupee.
+- **What it is.** `tools/make-hero-backdrop.mjs` writes it: halftone spheres, a starburst behind a light bulb that sits over the BrandMark lotus with its rupee, two banknotes, a coin stack, gears and a globe, in a teal-and-cream palette whose darkest ink is `#16322f`, not black. It uses no image, font, clock or random number, so two runs write the same bytes. The runnable check is `--check`, which exits 1 when the file on disk differs. It is 1200x630 (1.9:1) because `.ui-hero::before` scales it with `cover` and the filter chain was tuned on that shape. On a phone only the middle of the art shows.
+- **The CSS values did not change.** Opacity stays 0.40 (0.26 in the dark theme) and the filters `brightness(0.5) saturate(2.6) contrast(0.91)` (dark: `brightness(1.16) saturate(0.9)`). The comments above them were rewritten, because several claims in them were about the photo. One was imprecise even for the photo: `contrast` runs after `brightness` in the chain, so `contrast(0.91)` does not pull anything toward white. It maps a channel `c` to `0.91c + 0.045`, which adds about 0.045 to the darkest ink.
+- **Measured with `hero-art` (copy over the art).** Light: mean dL* 22, 100% present, 6.03:1 for the subtitle and the chips. Dark: 21.5, 95%, 6.81:1. Hindi at the large size: 21.7, 100%, 6.03:1. The photo gave 5.48:1 in the light theme and 6.80:1 in the dark one. So the art has a little more headroom than the photo and the same visibility.
+- **A one-off sweep, light theme, to price the headroom.** No `contrast()`: dL* 22.4, 5.79:1. `contrast(1.1)`: 22.9, 5.57:1. Opacity 0.46: 25.3, 5.17:1. Opacity 0.52: 28.8, 4.35:1 (below the 4.5:1 AA floor). Opacity 0.60: 33.2, 3.46:1. So the art could go to about 0.46, and "demo path over refactors" says it stays at 0.40. The sweep script lives outside the repo, and `index.css` was written back after each variant.
+- **Trap found by reading, not by a gate: the check and a Windows clone.** The first `--check` compared the file's text with the script's output byte for byte. With `core.autocrlf=true` a Windows checkout turns the SVG's LF into CRLF, so `--check` would have exited 1 on every fresh clone while the file was fine. It now ignores that difference. A one-off test (outside the repo) showed exit 0 for a CRLF copy, exit 1 for a copy with one attribute added and for an empty file, and the original bytes restored by a rewrite.
+- **`hero-vibrance.mjs` keeps its round-1 notes,** with a dated warning above them: they were measured on the photo, whose darkest dot was true black, and the new art has none.
+- **Gates run on the finished tree.** `make-hero-backdrop --check`, `svgcheck` (6 SVGs, 0 refused; the new one must be staged first, because it asks git), `hero-art` (3 modes, 0 problems), `hero-geom`, `hero-vibrance`, `brand-mark`, `parity4`, `keycheck`, `glyph-audit`, ESLint and the whole backend suite (2,189) with exit 0. The pages that sit on the art ran after that: `drive` (48 page loads and the hydrate check, 0 with problems), `mobile-audit` (50 loads across 5 phone widths, 0), `print-audit` (0 findings across 6 renders) and `lang-layout` (112 loads, 0), all exit 0. `load-perf` is left to the merged tree at the code freeze.
+- **Still open.** The photo is out of the working tree but stays in the git history from c92f907, and the repo is public. The MIT licence makes no promise about it, and a history rewrite would break every clone, so it was not done. If the owner finds the photo's source, `DATA_SOURCES.md` is the place to record it. The art is the author's own design and the owner has not said whether they like it.
