+### Talk first: the form's questions asked one at a time, aloud, 30 Oct 2026 (branch `feat/talk-first`)
+
+The owner's reply of 30 Oct said what "voice-only" meant: the readers are not technologically literate, so the default should be voice with some chat, and they should be able to speak their query, their address, their business idea and the other things the app needs, and get an answer back. Voice-only was declined earlier (see "Read aloud on the result pages"); this is the part that can be built honestly. The branch was cut from `feat/demo-polish` at `98976eb` and holds 11 commits, `03feae4` to `02bb3bc`. With the helper on, `/talk` asks the twelve form questions one at a time, aloud, and is the landing page's first button. The "answer" is what already existed, the rules' advice and the written plan; no EMI chatbot was added.
+
+- **Why the extract route needed a new field.** A bare "fifty thousand" or "no" belongs to no field unless the code says which question was on the screen. `ExtractRequest.asking` is one of the twelve fixed field names, validated by the schema; `_asking` adds one instruction to the prompt (an amount's "no" or "none" is 0, a reply that does not answer that question leaves the field out, rules 1 to 6 still apply). The model still only reads and quotes, and `validate_extraction` runs unchanged: an answer whose words are not in the transcript is dropped. What Meta receives grew by the name of the field asked, which `docs/PROCESSOR_SCHEDULE.md` §10 and `CLAUDE.md` now say.
+- **The order and the skipping.** `QUESTIONS` holds the twelve fields in `SENT_FIELDS` order, each with the dictionary key of its wording. A question is skipped when the form already holds an answer other than its starting value (`STARTS_AS`: debt is `'0'` and the area type is `'rural'`), or when the person has answered or skipped it on the page. Three wordings the form has no question for (debt, state, district) were added. In all, 11 `talk.*` keys were added and one (`talk.optional`) went, which makes 50 `talk.*` keys; the new ones are machine-written in all 8 languages and unreviewed (`docs/HINDI_REVIEW.md` has the eleven).
+- **Who is asked for what.** The first turn is still the open one (record or type anything, press Find my answers); skipping it starts the questions. In a guided turn the person records or types, the page transcribes and extracts in one go with `asking` set, shows what it found, and the person says "Yes, that is right", "No, I will say it again" or skips. `done` takes only the fields that landed on the form and the skipped ones; when nothing is left the page goes to the first unanswered form step, or `/finance`. A name and a business name are not sent, so they are not asked: the person types them on the first form step.
+- **The voice is a decided exception.** `promptVoiceFor` takes a voice on the device first and otherwise any voice of the page's language, and only for the page's own fixed question text; the result sentences keep `localVoiceFor`. The reason is the finding in the Listen entry above: every one of the 321 voices Edge listed on the owner's laptop was a network voice, so a device-only rule would leave the question voice with nothing on the machine that records the video. The text is ours and holds nothing the person said, but the browser's speech service sees it; `docs/PROCESSOR_SCHEDULE.md` §2, §10.2 and §10.8 record that, and `speak.js` says so in its header. The voice starts once the consent box is ticked and stops on Record or Find, when the box is unticked and when the page is left (S24 pins the last).
+- **The landing page.** With the helper on, the talk button is the first, primary one and the form button is secondary; the profile page keeps its "Rather talk than type?" card. With the helper off nothing changes. Four refuters cover it.
+- **How it was checked.** The backend went from 2,189 to 2,216 tests; `talk-lib-test` has 71, `speak-lib-test` 31. `talk-ui` now has 27 scenarios and 688 checks (S23 to S25 are the questions, the voice and the move on), and 39 planted defects each make it exit 1. The first run of them gave nine instrument faults (exit 2 where a finding was meant) and three the gate missed (the two review buttons stayed enabled while a request was out, and nothing checked the move on after the last answer); the gate was changed for each. `tools/README.md` has the table.
+- **What the code review found.** `/code-review medium 98976eb..HEAD` returned 8 findings. One was wrong: the `speak.js` header already documents the exception. A spoken "we have no household income" yields nothing, because rule 5 forbids a 0 for that field and `matchAnswers` drops one; the page says it found no answer and the person skips, so the household check reads "not checked" until they type 0 and tick the box on `/finance`. That is real and was not worth a new message (a key × 8 languages, a Hindi-sheet row and a gate scenario) on the demo path; it is written down in `docs/HACKATHON.md` §8 and the README. A real engine may drop the first utterance when `speak` follows `cancel` at once: `cancel()` is called only when something is playing, which is the guard the Listen entry settled on, and nothing here can show whether it is enough. The other five are by design or speculative.
+- **Still open.** No voice has been heard and the real model has not been asked with `asking` set: both are the rehearsal in `docs/HACKATHON.md` §10.1. Malayalam has no speech input (typing works) and whether any browser has a Malayalam voice to read the question has not been looked at. If the Bhashini key arrives, transcription moves to it on `feat/bhashini-speech`, and the extract step stays with Meta.
