+### Check these first: the conditions to read before the trip, 31 Oct 2026 (branch `feat/talk-first`)
+
+The owner's message of 30 Oct 2026 asked for "the 'Might not fit' notes". That was row 7 of the numbered feature table and had been cut earlier. The row's original wording could not be recovered (it is not in the repository, its history, the commit messages or the scratch folders that were searched), so the feature was rebuilt from its name, and the owner confirmed on 31 Oct 2026 that it is what row 7 meant. Two readings fit the name. One is a short list on each scheme of the conditions the scheme itself sets, under the line "might not fit you if one of these is not true". The other is a note when the reader's own figures sit outside a size the scheme states (a project cost over a cap, a loan under a floor). Only the first was built.
+
+- **Why conditions and not a verdict.** The form asks twelve questions and none is an age, a sex, a community, a trade or a membership, so nothing in the app can say that a scheme does not fit a person. Asking would add a request field, change the notice (a `NOTICE_VERSION` bump and a new test vector in the processor schedule) and mean evaluating eligibility rules, which the app has deliberately never done (`sc.eligUnchecked`, "No eligibility rule is evaluated anywhere"). So the block names the scheme's own conditions in the scheme's own words and says "might not fit", never "not eligible"; it prints no tick and no percentage.
+- **Which lines go in.** `CHECK_FIRST` in `backend/gen_schemes.py` maps a scheme id to the codes of the eligibility lines to show. A line goes in when it says who may apply (age, sex, community, residence, trade, membership, registration, a new unit), what kind of unit is covered, or whether the scheme is running at all. It stays out when a gate already cut the list by it (the states list, `location_types`) or when it only says what the scheme pays, charges or asks you to bring. 25 of the 30 schemes have an entry, 41 lines in all; the five that name nobody in particular (PMMY, DIC, the tehsil certificates, BLBC and the Gram Panchayat licence) have none.
+- **By code, never by position.** `eligibility_codes` and `eligibility_rules` are index-aligned, so a line inserted into one scheme would move a position onto the wrong sentence and still read fluently. The generator therefore writes `check_first_codes`, `scheme_service._check_first` looks each code up in the scheme's own pair of lists (and returns fresh lists, because the loaded file is shared), and the generator stops on an unknown scheme id, on a code the scheme does not carry and on a code named twice. The lines are the scheme's own sentences, so they reuse its `elig.*` and `common.*` translation codes and need no key of their own.
+- **Where it shows.** `/schemes` prints the block above the closed eligibility list, with the sentence "This scheme might not fit you if one of these is not true for you. We cannot check them for you." under the heading. `/report` prints it for every scheme in its one-line rows, because that page lists all of them. The small card on `/analysis` is unchanged on purpose. On `/report` two sentences can appear twice (PMEGP's period and PMFME's upgrade), because `SchemeFunding` already prints them as caveats of its funding figures; that was left alone. The only new keys are `sc.checkFirst` and `sc.checkFirstText`, in all 8 languages, machine-written. The author read the Hindi pair (rows 230 and 231 of `docs/HINDI_REVIEW.md`) on 31 Oct 2026 and left it as it is; the six other translations are unreviewed.
+- **How it was checked.** `test_check_first.py` (9 tests) was written first and failed before the code; the poison test of `scheme_service` covers six list fields now and `test_frontend_contract` checks the new keys. `drive` reads `/schemes` and `/report` in every case that has a result: the heading must print once per scheme that carries lines and every code at least as often as schemes carry it. Its console line shows `checkfirst=N` (9 on the good and nulls cases, 6 on green, none where there is no result), and a `case_good.json` from before the change stops it with a message to run `refresh-cases`, so it cannot pass without the check. Five planted defects each made it exit 1 (the block inside a closed `<details>`, the first line of every list dropped, the block gone from `/report`, the heading drawn for a scheme with no lines, and the `/report` guard loosened so that a result stored without the keys draws the heading), and every file was put back byte for byte (sha256). On the finished tree `drive` (48 loads), `lang-layout` (112), `mobile-audit` (50), `print-audit` (6 renders), `text-align` (33) and `scroll-audit` (38 hops) all exited 0 with no findings, and the backend went from 2,216 to 2,225 tests.
+- **The second reading was not built (the owner's call, 31 Oct 2026).** Size notes would be the first place the app compares the reader's figures with a scheme's own limits. That needs a table of numbers each pinned by a test to the scheme's prose, a carve-out in `sc.eligUnchecked` and in the rule above, and new coded sentences with parameters in 8 languages. It also overlaps the loan-offer card that `feat/advice-and-offer` builds in the same services and dictionaries, and the freeze is 3 Nov. If that changes: a `SIZE` table in the generator, `match_schemes` given the project cost and the funding gap, wording "about" and "might not fit", a separate commit, about two hours with the gates.
+- **The owner's answers, 31 Oct 2026.** (1) This is what row 7 meant. (2) The Hindi of the two new strings is right. (3) The three notes about a scheme's period or a new unit stay (PMFME's, PMEGP's and the third of Stand-Up India's), although they fit "if one of these is not true for you" only loosely. (4) The size notes are not built.
+- **Still open.** The six other translations (Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Kannada, Marathi, Bengali) are machine-written and no native speaker has read them; the Hindi pair was read by one Hindi reader.
