+- **Candidate 1, what the figure is.** `advisory.amounts` is an object keyed by the translation code of each sentence the page shows (the entries of `recommendation_codes` and the next step's code), so a sentence with no entry has no figure and the page draws only what the backend sent. Six codes get one, of four kinds: `break_even` (`adv.reduceExpenses`, `nxt.reviewExpenses`: the monthly shortfall, rounded up), `loan_payments` (`nxt.reducePressure`: the loan payments that still read "low", with the estimated loan for the funding gap counted in as the pressure figure counts it; `with_new_loan` says so), `household_payments` (`adv.householdAlreadyOver`, `nxt.householdOverLimit`: the payments the household check would not call over its limit) and `cash_reserve` (`adv.cashReserve`: the shortfall to ₹10,000, which is this app's own mark and not a published rule; it was a bare `10000` in `advisory_service.py` and is now `CASH_RESERVE_RUPEES`). `amounts_service.py` has no rule of its own: it runs the rule that raised the sentence backwards. The three sentences about the size of a new loan (`adv.householdOverLimit`, `nxt.householdNewLoanOver`, `nxt.gapTooLarge`) are left to the loan options, which already give that number, and `adv.improveMargin` has no figure in this version.
+- **Found by searching, not by solving.** A limit is the largest payment, in whole rupees, for which the page's own function (`calculate_repayment_pressure`) still gives the verdict, found by halving; a cut is rounded the safe way, so doing what the line says is always enough. Exact arithmetic would offer a limit that the page then contradicts: 30,000 against a surplus of 100,001 is 29.9997%, which prints as 30% and is called moderate, so the limit there is 29,995 (`test_the_limit_follows_the_rounded_figure_the_page_prints_not_exact_30_percent`).
+- **The sentences are unchanged.** They carry no digit, so the read-aloud (which leaves out a line with a digit) and the written plan (which drops an advice sentence with a digit) keep them, and no amount reaches Meta (`test_no_amount_reaches_the_written_plan`). The figure is its own line under the sentence on `/analysis`, `/advisory` and `/report` (on the last two under the first five recommendations; the next step's figure on `/analysis` only), in Simple as well as Detailed, and is 5 `amt.*` keys × 8 languages (`amt.loanPaymentsNew` when the loan for the funding gap is counted). A result stored before the amounts existed draws none. The demo input (tailoring, Lucknow) has no sentence with a figure and so shows no amount line.
+- **Checks for candidate 1.** `test_advice_amounts.py` has 19 tests. The last is a sweep of 1,440 inputs through `run_unified_analysis`: for every entry the limit passes the real rules, one rupee more does not, and the cut reaches the limit; no sentence has gained a digit; and all four kinds, all six codes and both values of `with_new_loan` are reached. `test_model_boundary` went from 24 to 25 cases (the module may import neither the Meta client nor anything that opens a connection). On the browser side: `amount-lib-test.mjs` (17 `node --test` tests of the sentence builder), the `drive` check, which works out from each response which lines each page should print and under which sentence, on every page and in Simple too (3 looks), and two real cases added to `refresh-cases.mjs` (`pressure` and `household`), the only ones that reach the loan-payment kinds.
+- **Candidate 3, what the card is.** On `/advisory`, after the Detailed-only block and before the written-plan card, and only with a result: Simple keeps it and print leaves it out. The reader says how the offer was told, types what they were told and sees what they get in hand, what they pay back in all, what it costs a year, the same money at the rate this app assumes for its own loans (read from the result, 12% a year; "not a quote from any lender") and the loan the rules priced for them. It is JavaScript that runs in the tab: `data/loanOffer.js` does the sums, `i18n/offerLines.js` picks the sentences and `components/OfferCheck.jsx` draws them. Nothing is sent, nothing is stored (the boxes are component state, gone with the page) and no model is involved; the CLAUDE.md paragraph on the model's job says so, and names the other sum the browser does (the running summary on step 4).
+- **The two ways, and what the yearly figure is.** *Instalments*: the money in hand after any fee, each payment, how often (every day, week or month) and how many. The yearly cost is the rate per payment at which the payments, taken as they fall due, are worth the money in hand (found by halving an interval 200 times, with `expm1` and `log1p` so that a very small rate stays exact), times 365, 52 or 12. It is a nominal rate: interest is not added on interest, and a daily offer counts 365 payments a year, so for a lender who does not collect every day it is an upper bound (the card says so). *Rate*: so many rupees for every 100 each month, and the months. The interest is taken on the whole amount for the whole time and the amount is paid back at the end, which is the usual reading of such a quote and an assumption the card states; the yearly cost is the rate times 12. A fee taken out first is not counted. The rupee extra of this way is worked out exactly in `BigInt` and rounded half a rupee up. The card calls the yearly figure "a cost of about N% a year" and nowhere says APR (no check watches for that word).
+- **It gives no opinion.** The card says what an offer costs and sets it beside the assumed rate. It never says an offer is illegal, too high, fair or unfair, and never tells anyone to take or to leave one. `tools/verify/offer-banned.mjs` lists, for each of the 8 languages, the words no sentence of the card may use, matched from the start of a word (so the Malayalam for "than the one shown", which holds the letters of the word for deceit, is not a match). Both `offer-lines-test` and the browser gate (`BANNED-WORD`) read it. It is a guard against a slip and not a proof: a sentence can mislead without using a listed word.
+- **No choice is made for the reader.** Neither way and no frequency is selected until the reader selects it, because a guess would be priced as an answer. What is typed for one way is kept apart from the other's, because the money in hand after a fee is not the money borrowed. The boxes are text, not number inputs, because the sums read the digits as typed and the card says what is wrong with them; the keyboard is still a numeric one. Commas, signs, letters, non-ASCII digits and values over the caps are refused with a sentence (caps: ₹100 crore, ten years of payments, that is 3,650 daily, 520 weekly or 120 monthly, 120 months, and a rate of 100).
